{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_El5NNaBcHuxr8RHw33L1SFbR","content":"diff --git a/src/app.py b/src/app.py\nindex db49c5e..f4c39cc 100644\n--- a/src/app.py\n+++ b/src/app.py\n@@ -22,12 +22,12 @@ def words(text):\n \n \n def similarity(a, b):\n-    \"\"\"Shared-tag ratio (Jaccard), blended with shared words of the two bodies.\"\"\"\n+    \"\"\"Shared-tag ratio (Jaccard), blended with shared words, as a percentage.\"\"\"\n     ta, tb = set(a.get(\"tags\", [])), set(b.get(\"tags\", []))\n     tag_score = len(ta & tb) / len(ta | tb) if (ta | tb) else 0\n     wa, wb = words(a.get(\"body\")), words(b.get(\"body\"))\n     word_score = len(wa & wb) / len(wa | wb) if (wa | wb) else 0\n-    return round(0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score, 4)\n+    return round(100 * (0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score))\n \n \n def related(story, existing):\ndiff --git a/tests/test_publish.py b/tests/test_publish.py\nindex 6c9ac44..d84e88b 100644\n--- a/tests/test_publish.py\n+++ b/tests/test_publish.py\n@@ -23,6 +23,29 @@ class PublishTest(unittest.TestCase):\n         self.assertEqual(table.get_item(Key={\"slug\": \"why-my-cron-ran-twice\"})[\"Item\"][\"title\"], \"Why my cron ran twice\")\n         self.assertEqual(table.scan()[\"Count\"], 1)\n \n+    def test_publish_stores_integer_scores_with_existing_story(self):\n+        table = FakeTable()\n+        table.put_item(Item={\n+            \"slug\": \"first-story\",\n+            \"title\": \"First story\",\n+            \"tags\": [\"cron\"],\n+            \"body\": \"Cron ran twice.\",\n+            \"published_at\": 1,\n+            \"similar\": [],\n+        })\n+        story = {\n+            \"slug\": \"second-story\",\n+            \"title\": \"Second story\",\n+            \"tags\": [\"cron\"],\n+            \"body\": \"Cron ran twice.\",\n+        }\n+\n+        stored = app.publish(table, story)\n+\n+        self.assertEqual(stored[\"similar\"], [{\"slug\": \"first-story\", \"score\": 100}])\n+        self.assertIsInstance(stored[\"similar\"][0][\"score\"], int)\n+        self.assertEqual(table.scan()[\"Count\"], 2)\n+\n \n if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n     unittest.main()\n"}
{"role":"assistant","content":"Both publishes now return 201, the stored score is an integer, and both tests pass. I’ll document the root cause, the boundary conversion, the seeded regression test, and exactly which parts of the colleague’s note were used.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_Xz5ShrI0fWgXQkdA5yAgY5XR","function":{"name":"Write","arguments":"{\"path\":\"ANSWER.md\",\"content\":\"# Cause\\n\\n`similarity()` in `src/app.py` used division and returned `round(...)` with a precision argument, which is a Python `float`. `publish()` placed that value in `similar[].score`, and DynamoDB's boto3 serializer rejects floats with `TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`\\n\\nThe first publish succeeded because the table was empty, so `related()` returned an empty list and no float reached `put_item`. The second publish found an existing story, computed a float score, and failed; `handler()` converted that exception into the observed 500 response. The original test only exercised the empty-table path.\\n\\n# Fix\\n\\n- `src/app.py` now returns similarity as a whole-number integer percentage from 0 to 100. DynamoDB accepts Python integers, and the relative ordering of scores is unchanged.\\n- `tests/test_publish.py` now includes a regression test that seeds the table before publishing. It exercises the populated-table path and verifies the stored related-story score is the integer `100`, so reintroducing a float will fail the test.\\n\\nVerification: `python3 -m unittest discover tests` passes both tests. Calling the real `handler()` twice locally also returns 201 for both requests, with an integer similarity score stored for the second story.\\n\\n# Colleague's note\\n\\nApplied: the float-at-the-DynamoDB-boundary diagnosis, the explanation for why only the second write fails, conversion to integer percentages, and adding a seeded-table write test.\\n\\nNot applied: converting scores to `Decimal` was unnecessary because the integer representation is sufficient. No real AWS table or deployment was used; the existing fake table reproduces boto3's type validation locally.\\n\"}"}}]}
